WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.234
-Our next guest is a talented
director, writer, and actor

00:00:03.234 --> 00:00:04.467
you know from movies like
"Clerks,"

00:00:04.467 --> 00:00:06.868
"Jay and Silent Bob
Strike Back," and "Dogma."

00:00:06.868 --> 00:00:09.467
His new show, "Masters of the
Universe: Revelation,"

00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:10.467
is streaming on Netflix.

00:00:10.467 --> 00:00:11.501
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.501 --> 00:00:14.868
-Grayskull is not
the prize I'm after.

00:00:14.868 --> 00:00:16.567
♪♪

00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:20.100
This castle is merely a shell.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.801
The pearl I seek
is trapped inside.

00:00:23.801 --> 00:00:26.801
So I'm not here
to take the castle.

00:00:26.801 --> 00:00:29.968
I've come to
tear Grayskull down,

00:00:29.968 --> 00:00:32.501
stone by stone!

00:00:32.501 --> 00:00:35.000
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Kevin Smith.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.534
How you are, Kevin?

00:00:37.534 --> 00:00:39.133
-I am excellent, sir.

00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:42.934
We are coming to you live
from the set of "Clerks 3."

00:00:42.934 --> 00:00:45.367
No, I don't always
dress like this,

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:47.200
only when we're making
a movie and stuff.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.067
Brian O'Halloran with me.
Jason Mewes here.

00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:52.434
I got the whole
cast and crew behind me.

00:00:52.434 --> 00:00:54.734
And we got a
special message for you, man,

00:00:54.734 --> 00:00:57.267
because in Hollywood,
you just burn money and time.

00:00:57.267 --> 00:00:59.901
And we had so much of both,
we did this.

00:00:59.901 --> 00:01:02.467
Ready? Watch this.
Count of three, everybody.

00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:04.734
One, two, three.

00:01:04.734 --> 00:01:07.501
-Hi, Seth Meyers!

00:01:07.501 --> 00:01:09.801
-Wow!
-Alright, everybody go to lunch.

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:12.734
Thank you very much.
-Bye, Brian. Bye, Jason.

00:01:12.734 --> 00:01:15.000
That was a -- That's like --
It's like looking at

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.667
a "Clerks" renaissance fair.

00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:19.033
-It really is,
to a large degree.

00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:20.267
Could you hold
the mic real quick?

00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:22.100
I just wanted to show
you this feature, okay?

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.901
Like, this is me as Silent Bob.
-Yeah.

00:01:23.901 --> 00:01:27.334
-Watch how easy -- Like,
this is movie magic right here.

00:01:27.334 --> 00:01:29.434
-Yeah.
-Here we go.

00:01:29.434 --> 00:01:31.968
Just like that.
-Wow! Kevin!

00:01:31.968 --> 00:01:34.200
It's Kevin, guys.
He is here.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.300
-I know -- you couldn't
recognize me before,

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.300
but this is -- I appreciate you
doing this, man,

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.334
via Zoom and whatnot,

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:44.801
because something
happened to me a few months ago

00:01:44.801 --> 00:01:47.167
and I said --
I instantly called my agent

00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:49.701
and said please book me on
"Seth Meyers."

00:01:49.701 --> 00:01:51.601
Because I watch
the show all the time,

00:01:51.601 --> 00:01:53.067
and in my head and heart,

00:01:53.067 --> 00:01:54.100
we're, like, good friends.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.667
And I know we're not, but, like,

00:01:56.667 --> 00:01:59.601
nothing happens to me
unless I share it with you.

00:01:59.601 --> 00:02:01.534
So, something happened
to me at the periodontist,

00:02:01.534 --> 00:02:02.567
and I called up my agent.

00:02:02.567 --> 00:02:04.334
I said can you
book me on "Meyers"?

00:02:04.334 --> 00:02:05.901
And it was like months ago.
Months ago.

00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:07.234
But I was like, "I'll wait."

00:02:07.234 --> 00:02:10.000
And then it happened,
but we were making "Clerks 3,"

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.367
which I've been trying to
make for seven years.

00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:15.501
So the logic was,
cancel out on "Meyers,"

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:16.701
but I was like,
"What are you, mad?"

00:02:16.701 --> 00:02:17.934
Like, it doesn't work.

00:02:17.934 --> 00:02:19.334
Nothing happens unless
it happens on "Meyers."

00:02:19.334 --> 00:02:21.167
So, even though I've been
trying to make this movie

00:02:21.167 --> 00:02:23.701
seven years, I said,
"Everything goes on hold

00:02:23.701 --> 00:02:25.367
so I can do this interview,"
man, because it means the world.

00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:27.667
-And I just want to tell you how
excited I am to hear this story,

00:02:27.667 --> 00:02:28.968
because a lot of people
want to come on

00:02:28.968 --> 00:02:30.601
and they want to plug a project.

00:02:30.601 --> 00:02:32.701
But the fact that you're here to
plug the fact that you went to

00:02:32.701 --> 00:02:35.767
the periodontist
means the world to me.

00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:36.901
-Well, first,
before I even say that,

00:02:36.901 --> 00:02:39.000
can I tell you a
quick story about the wife?

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.334
So, you know I'm a fan,
and so I watched "Lobby Baby"

00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:44.634
by myself late at night,
smoking weed,

00:02:44.634 --> 00:02:45.634
and it was amazing.

00:02:45.634 --> 00:02:47.701
And it was so good
that I was like,

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:49.367
"I think my wife
will get into this."

00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:52.934
Because quite like you, as a
comedian, you prop up your wife.

00:02:52.934 --> 00:02:54.801
You're not one of those
"I can't believe I'm married."

00:02:54.801 --> 00:02:56.701
You're like, "I can't believe
she married me."

00:02:56.701 --> 00:02:58.501
So, I said, "She is going to
love this special."

00:02:58.501 --> 00:03:00.601
So, I sit down with my wife
and watch the special,

00:03:00.601 --> 00:03:01.901
and she absolutely loves it.

00:03:01.901 --> 00:03:03.000
But this is her review.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.434
This is what she says
at one point.

00:03:04.434 --> 00:03:06.934
She goes,
"Oh, his wife is so lucky.

00:03:06.934 --> 00:03:09.200
He's funny and cute."

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.734
And at that point, I'm like,
"What?

00:03:11.734 --> 00:03:14.167
So what? I'm just funny?"

00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:16.133
And she's like,
"Well, sometimes.

00:03:16.133 --> 00:03:17.634
He's funny all the time."

00:03:17.634 --> 00:03:20.534
And at that point, even though,
like, I consider you my buddy,

00:03:20.534 --> 00:03:22.167
I threw shade right away, man,

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:24.567
'cause I was like, "Look,
the Meyerses don't have it all.

00:03:24.567 --> 00:03:26.033
Have you ever seen their dog?

00:03:26.033 --> 00:03:29.400
Their dog -- it looks like the
dog that Frasier's --

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.834
Maris got, you know?"

00:03:30.834 --> 00:03:33.567
Like, you never saw Maris,
but you did see the dog.

00:03:33.567 --> 00:03:35.133
But I was like -- You know,

00:03:35.133 --> 00:03:37.601
I'm trying to make "Frasier"
the new "M-A-S-H",

00:03:37.601 --> 00:03:40.667
If not the new "Star Trek:
The Next Generation."

00:03:40.667 --> 00:03:42.167
Alright,
here's my periodontist story.

00:03:42.167 --> 00:03:45.400
So, I just turned 51, right?
But I was 50 when this happened.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:46.634
It was a few months ago.

00:03:46.634 --> 00:03:48.767
So, I went to the dentist
to get a cleaning.

00:03:48.767 --> 00:03:50.834
I thought I hadn't, like,
had a cleaning in two years.

00:03:50.834 --> 00:03:52.767
And I got in the room.
He was like, "Seven.

00:03:52.767 --> 00:03:54.367
You haven't had
a cleaning in seven years."

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:55.367
And I was like "Alright."

00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:56.734
So, he goes to take --

00:03:56.734 --> 00:03:58.434
-He goes, "I've been trying to
have you get a cleaning

00:03:58.434 --> 00:04:01.334
since you've been trying to make
the next 'Clerks.'"

00:04:01.334 --> 00:04:04.000
-Pretty much.
Pretty much the same time.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.934
So, he takes pictures
of my mouth.

00:04:05.934 --> 00:04:07.868
He leaves the room --
never a good sign.

00:04:07.868 --> 00:04:09.567
Then he comes back in
and he goes,

00:04:09.567 --> 00:04:11.801
"Well, I got bad news
and worse news."

00:04:11.801 --> 00:04:13.567
I was like, "Oh, what do I got?
Gingivitis?"

00:04:13.567 --> 00:04:15.567
He goes,
"You wish you had gingivitis."

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:17.467
He's like,
"You got periodontal disease."

00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:18.868
I was like,
"What you are talking about?"

00:04:18.868 --> 00:04:20.200
He's like, "Yeah, man,
your mouth is just rotting

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:21.501
under your jawline.

00:04:21.501 --> 00:04:23.167
We got to go to work.
We got to pull some teeth."

00:04:23.167 --> 00:04:24.467
And I'm like,
"What are you talking about?"

00:04:24.467 --> 00:04:26.100
He's like, "It's just --
It's periodontal disease.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.234
Irreversible.

00:04:27.234 --> 00:04:28.400
You'll have it
for the rest of your life.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.033
It's like
luggage or herpes, man.

00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:31.067
You're going to
carry it forever."

00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:32.200
And stuff like that.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:33.367
So, I said,
"Man, how did this happen?"

00:04:33.367 --> 00:04:34.667
I was cursing myself.

00:04:34.667 --> 00:04:36.501
I was like, "I should have
brushed my teeth more."

00:04:36.501 --> 00:04:38.767
And that's when the periodontist
let me off the hook.

00:04:38.767 --> 00:04:39.868
He's like, "Listen, man,

00:04:39.868 --> 00:04:41.934
you could have brushed
all you wanted.

00:04:41.934 --> 00:04:44.634
You were always going to be
here in my office one day."

00:04:44.634 --> 00:04:46.934
And I said "Why?"
And he goes, "This is genetics."

00:04:46.934 --> 00:04:48.334
And right away,
I got to blame my mom.

00:04:48.334 --> 00:04:50.701
I called her up and I was like,
"You gave me bad hips --

00:04:50.701 --> 00:04:53.501
big child-bearing hips,
a bad heart, and a bad mouth."

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:55.601
So, I said, "What do you mean,
like, genetic?"

00:04:55.601 --> 00:04:58.033
And he goes, "Basically,
it's because of your parents.

00:04:58.033 --> 00:05:01.133
Your mouth is hard at work

00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:03.567
because you have
a hyperimmune system."

00:05:03.567 --> 00:05:04.868
And I was like,
"What are you talking about?

00:05:04.868 --> 00:05:06.033
What is that?"

00:05:06.033 --> 00:05:07.901
He goes, "It's exactly
what it sounds like.

00:05:07.901 --> 00:05:10.334
Basically, like, whenever
you get a little something,

00:05:10.334 --> 00:05:12.300
your body goes after it
in a big, bad way."

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.300
He was like,
"Do you get sick often?"

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:14.334
I said "No.

00:05:14.334 --> 00:05:15.734
I'm kind of like Bruce Willis in

00:05:15.734 --> 00:05:17.634
that one
M. Night Shyamalan movie."

00:05:17.634 --> 00:05:19.667
And he was like, "Alright.
So, which one?"

00:05:19.667 --> 00:05:20.868
And I said,
"You know, 'Unbreakable.'"

00:05:20.868 --> 00:05:24.067
He goes, "Oof, thank the Lord.
Not, you know --"

00:05:24.067 --> 00:05:25.434
-Not "Sixth Sense," right.

00:05:25.434 --> 00:05:27.868
-Thank you very much. Totally.
-Not where you're dead.

00:05:27.868 --> 00:05:29.200
-So, in any event, man,

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.300
I was just like, "So,
what are we looking at here?"

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:34.634
And he's like, "The idea is,
when your body

00:05:34.634 --> 00:05:37.501
encounters a foreign substance,
it attacks it

00:05:37.501 --> 00:05:39.934
with everything as if, like,
you're defending yourself

00:05:39.934 --> 00:05:42.067
against a mouse,
but with a bazooka."

00:05:42.067 --> 00:05:43.367
And I said,
"Well, is that good?"

00:05:43.367 --> 00:05:44.901
And he goes, "Yeah, because,
do you ever get sick?"

00:05:44.901 --> 00:05:46.767
And I was like, "Not really."
And he goes, "That's good."

00:05:46.767 --> 00:05:49.067
He's going, "The only problem
is, in your mouth, there's been

00:05:49.067 --> 00:05:50.901
this war that's going on
your entire life."

00:05:50.901 --> 00:05:52.234
And I said "Why?"

00:05:52.234 --> 00:05:55.200
And he goes, "Because you have
bacteria in your body

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.100
that your body
has been fighting,

00:05:57.100 --> 00:06:00.300
and it's fighting so hard,
it's chewing out your jawline."

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.000
And I was like,
"What is this bacteria?"

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.067
And he goes,
"It's a part of your biofilm.

00:06:04.067 --> 00:06:06.567
It's been with you
since you were born."

00:06:06.567 --> 00:06:08.200
Instantly,
when he says "biofilm,"

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:09.367
I'm like,
"I got to talk to Meyers, man.

00:06:09.367 --> 00:06:10.501
Has he ever heard of this?

00:06:10.501 --> 00:06:11.734
'Cause he's, like,
the smartest guy I know."

00:06:11.734 --> 00:06:13.367
-It makes me so happy

00:06:13.367 --> 00:06:15.334
that you thought
about me during this time.

00:06:15.334 --> 00:06:16.467
-While this was going on.

00:06:16.467 --> 00:06:17.767
While a man was in my mouth,

00:06:17.767 --> 00:06:18.901
I was thinking about
Seth Meyers.

00:06:18.901 --> 00:06:19.968
It's that simple.

00:06:19.968 --> 00:06:22.300
So, I said,
"What is this biofilm?"

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.934
He goes,
"Well, when you're in utero,"

00:06:24.934 --> 00:06:27.067
he's going,
"you're completely germ-free.

00:06:27.067 --> 00:06:28.534
You're in a germ-free
environment.

00:06:28.534 --> 00:06:30.133
But the moment you're born,

00:06:30.133 --> 00:06:31.801
bacteria is introduced
to your system.

00:06:31.801 --> 00:06:33.167
That is how you
build your immune system.

00:06:33.167 --> 00:06:35.634
And that bacteria
is part of your biofilm.

00:06:35.634 --> 00:06:37.167
You carry it forever" and stuff.

00:06:37.167 --> 00:06:38.434
And I was like,
"Well, what happens?

00:06:38.434 --> 00:06:39.868
Is it like when
you come out the chute,

00:06:39.868 --> 00:06:41.000
like, you hit the air

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.400
and you breathe them
for the first time?"

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:43.534
And he goes, "Oh, no, no.

00:06:43.534 --> 00:06:45.234
The bacteria you pick up,

00:06:45.234 --> 00:06:48.000
you pick up in
the mother's birth canal."

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.200
And, so, I was like,
"Wait a second.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.901
You're telling me
I'm at the periodontist

00:06:50.901 --> 00:06:52.501
and I got to get
teeth ripped out

00:06:52.501 --> 00:06:54.000
because my mother
is still in my mouth?"

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:55.334
And he's like, "Oh, yeah.

00:06:55.334 --> 00:06:58.434
You've been fighting
that since you were born, Kev."

00:06:58.434 --> 00:07:00.267
And I said,
"This hyperimmune system --

00:07:00.267 --> 00:07:01.367
it couldn't do anything for me?"

00:07:01.367 --> 00:07:03.434
I said, "Like,
am I like Wolverine?"

00:07:03.434 --> 00:07:04.801
And he goes,
"Well, wait a second.

00:07:04.801 --> 00:07:06.100
Do you have abs?"

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:08.701
And I lifted up my shirt,
and, Seth, I don't have any.

00:07:08.701 --> 00:07:10.667
I don't have ab one,
let alone plural.

00:07:10.667 --> 00:07:12.901
So, I said "No."
And I said "Why?"

00:07:12.901 --> 00:07:14.767
And he goes, "Well, I think of
Wolverine, I think of abs."

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:15.868
He's going,
"But you don't have abs,

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:17.467
so you're
definitely not Wolverine."

00:07:17.467 --> 00:07:20.167
And he went in and took the
teeth out and stuff like that.

00:07:20.167 --> 00:07:22.968
P.S., the story though,
before I had the procedure,

00:07:22.968 --> 00:07:24.200
he says this, man.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:26.200
He goes, "Listen,
this is going to be five hours,

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:27.300
and we put you under."

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:28.667
I said, "I can't wait."

00:07:28.667 --> 00:07:31.000
And he goes, "But the problem
is, that's a long time.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:32.701
So you might have
to go the bathroom."

00:07:32.701 --> 00:07:34.567
And I was like, "I'll go before
we -- the operation."

00:07:34.567 --> 00:07:36.901
He goes,
"Well, you could do that."

00:07:36.901 --> 00:07:39.200
He goes, "Or we have
something you could use."

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:40.300
And I'm thinking like,

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:42.000
"Are they going to
catheterize me for this?

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:43.634
Like, is there a tube
or something like that?"

00:07:43.634 --> 00:07:44.734
I said,
"What you are talking about?"

00:07:44.734 --> 00:07:46.267
And he goes, "Listen, you know,

00:07:46.267 --> 00:07:47.901
we start our lives
wearing these.

00:07:47.901 --> 00:07:50.067
Sometimes, we end our lives
wearing these."

00:07:50.067 --> 00:07:52.334
And I was like, "Are you
offering me an adult diaper

00:07:52.334 --> 00:07:53.601
to get my teeth worked on?"

00:07:53.601 --> 00:07:56.200
He goes, "No shame whatsoever.
Just let it flow."

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:57.400
I said, "I think I can hold it."

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:00.434
And, Seth, like a big boy,
I held it.

00:08:00.434 --> 00:08:03.434
-I -- On behalf of
everyone here,

00:08:03.434 --> 00:08:05.567
we're so proud of you.

00:08:05.567 --> 00:08:09.901
-Thank you. Is Scollins proud?

00:08:09.901 --> 00:08:13.567
-Scollins is fully tuned out
at this part of the show.

00:08:13.567 --> 00:08:16.267
But I want to say, though,
we've run out of time.

00:08:16.267 --> 00:08:18.801
You've done a wonderful job
plugging periodontistry.

00:08:18.801 --> 00:08:20.534
And -- But I want to say
a few things.

00:08:20.534 --> 00:08:21.767
And I do want you to
come back soon,

00:08:21.767 --> 00:08:23.901
'cause "Masters of the Universe"

00:08:23.901 --> 00:08:25.300
you've done
a great job adapting.

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:27.567
You have a new book that I think
everybody should read.

00:08:27.567 --> 00:08:29.767
I'd like to talk about these.
I'd like you to come back.

00:08:29.767 --> 00:08:31.434
I'm really happy
you're doing "Clerks 3."

00:08:31.434 --> 00:08:32.767
It's always such a pleasure.

00:08:32.767 --> 00:08:33.934
And I still have your weed,

00:08:33.934 --> 00:08:35.767
and one day,
we're going to smoke it, okay?

00:08:35.767 --> 00:08:37.868
-That means the world, man,
that it's still there.

00:08:37.868 --> 00:08:40.734
I watch every episode
to see if it's in the cup

00:08:40.734 --> 00:08:42.734
the way Howard Shore
watches every episode of

00:08:42.734 --> 00:08:43.801
"Saturday Night Live"
to make sure

00:08:43.801 --> 00:08:45.133
they're still using
his theme music.

00:08:45.133 --> 00:08:47.167
So I've been watching.
Thank you for carrying it.

00:08:47.167 --> 00:08:49.434
-You're not getting any --
whatever -- residuals.

00:08:49.434 --> 00:08:51.467
"Masters of the Universe:
Revelation"

00:08:51.467 --> 00:08:52.467
is streaming on Netflix.

